<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koine Greek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as koine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koine Greek was the common form of Greek that spread across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> after the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. It served as the language of administration, commerce and culture in the Hellenistic kingdoms, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. Its spread was one of the most lasting legacies of Seleucid rule, and it later became the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koine-greek.jpg" alt="Koine Greek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_Script_from_Rosetta_Stone_(36140196440).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
